<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Olynthus</h1><p class="st-meta">479 BCE – 348 BCE · capital Olynthus</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chief city of Chalcidice, head of the Chalcidian League, whose destruction by Philip II in 348 BCE left the best-preserved classical Greek town plan.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Olynthus stood on hills near the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-kassandra">Toronaic Gulf</a>. It was a town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bottiaeans">Bottiaeans</a> until 479 BCE, when the Persian commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artabazus-i-of-phrygia">Artabazus</a> massacred its people and handed it to Chalcidian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> under Critobulus of Torone. In 432 BCE many Chalcidians abandoned their coastal towns to settle there, at the urging of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas-ii-of-macedon">Perdiccas II of Macedon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Olynthus became the centre of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcidian-league">Chalcidian League</a>, a federal state with shared citizenship and coinage of fine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. A new town on a regular grid of blocks was built on the North Hill; excavations led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-moore-robinson">David Robinson</a> in the 1920s and 1930s uncovered whole houses with courtyards, mosaics and household goods, a rich picture of daily life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a> forced the league to submit after a war in 382-379 BCE. Restored, Olynthus became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a>'s main rival in the north. Despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes">Demosthenes</a>' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olynthiacs">Olynthiac speeches</a> urging Athenian help, Philip took the city by treachery in 348 BCE, razed it and sold its people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/olynthus.jpg" alt="Olynthus" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Hermann Hammer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bouleuterion_in_Olynthos_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>479 BCE – 348 BCE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Olynthus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Greece</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Olynthos</dd></dl></aside></div>

